WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:04.720
Shanghai residents are finally out on the streets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.760
celebrating the end of a&nbsp;
strict two-month lockdown.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.760
But it might not be the last time&nbsp;
they have to go through this.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.440
As China clings to what it calls&nbsp;
a “dynamic zero-COVID strategy,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.160
people can face stringent lockdowns any time&nbsp;
there’s a rise in cases where they live.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.800
In Shanghai, residents were basically&nbsp;
locked up at home for weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.400
Shops, schools and offices were closed. The&nbsp;
lives of 25 million people were put on hold.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.560
The social and economic consequences of&nbsp;
these tough restrictions have been painful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.640
And the suffering might seem very unnecessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.800
especially when the rest of the world&nbsp;
has basically returned to normal.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.840
So why is China sticking to its zero-COVID policy?

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:13.920
The answer is that it has little choice.&nbsp;
China's failure to capitalize on its early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.680
pandemic success and adapt to different&nbsp;
circumstances today means it's now stuck.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.840
To understand why, we need to go back&nbsp;
to the very beginning of the pandemic.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.920
Remember Wuhan, the city where the coronavirus&nbsp;
was first found? It was also the first city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:41.520
in the world to enter a COVID lockdown. And by&nbsp;
December 2020, while most countries were still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.920
battling devastating outbreaks, life for the&nbsp;
city’s 11 million people was back to normal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.400
That’s thanks to a strict lockdown that&nbsp;
was imposed right after the COVID outbreak.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.200
In China, their first objective is to keep the&nbsp;
virus out of the community as much as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.800
with travel restrictions. And then if&nbsp;
the virus does get in, which we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.680
will happen sooner or later when there's an&nbsp;
outbreak in a particular community, [it] does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.880
very stringent control measures:&nbsp;
case finding, isolation of cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.440
contact tracing and quarantine. And that's&nbsp;
facilitated as well typically by lockdowns.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.080
Professor Cowling is an epidemiologist based&nbsp;
in Hong Kong – where pandemic measures have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:32.720
been mostly in line with mainland China. The city&nbsp;
has been pretty much COVID-free until recently.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:39.200
It's a very efficient strategy compared to other&nbsp;
strategies, because as long as most of the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:46.240
can be spent at zero daily cases, actually life&nbsp;
can be pretty normal inside cities in China.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.640
In fact, China’s initial&nbsp;
approach was so effective that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.560
it was regarded internationally as&nbsp;
a successful example to learn from.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Professor Jane Duckett spent&nbsp;
18 months with her team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.600
funded by the UK government, looking into&nbsp;
China’s pandemic strategy back in 2020.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.880
I think it was successful in the beginning,&nbsp;
obviously, in the sense that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:16.320
got infections and deaths, death rates back&nbsp;
down to zero from around late March 2020.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.000
But what was effective then is now less so.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.200
With the arrival of the more transmissible&nbsp;
Omicron variant, it has become much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.400
difficult to stop the spread of the virus&nbsp;
– even with measures like lockdowns.&nbsp;

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.760
Countries like Singapore and New Zealand, which&nbsp;
initially adopted their own zero-COVID policies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.520
have changed tack and are now&nbsp;
trying to ‘live with’ the virus.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.520
But China has not. Its success story back in 2020&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:55.840
has arguably become the biggest hurdle in the&nbsp;
way of a more flexible approach today, in 2022.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.440
A major reason for China’s hesitation to open up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.880
is that the vaccination rate&nbsp;
among its elderly population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:11.440
is exceptionally low – and that’s largely due&nbsp;
to the consistently low number of infections.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.840
I think in a sense the successful containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.320
made the vaccination, especially&nbsp;
of older people, seem less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.720
necessary. Right? If you don't have it spreading&nbsp;
through society, you don't seem to need vaccines.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:29.120
Only 19.7% percent of people above the age of&nbsp;
80 in China have received a vaccination booster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.160
while just over 50% of them have&nbsp;
completed their primary vaccinations.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.760
I suppose you could say they trusted&nbsp;
the government to protect them…As long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:43.600
as the zero-COVID strategy continues, then the&nbsp;
public health measures are limiting infections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:50.640
And so vaccines would be like a backup plan, but&nbsp;
maybe not an urgent priority for older adults.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.120
A recent study published by Shanghai’s&nbsp;
Fudan University claims that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.400
with the currently low vaccination&nbsp;
rate among elderly people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.480
the lifting of the zero-COVID policy&nbsp;
could lead to over 1.5 million deaths.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.880
China could have pushed for a more aggressive&nbsp;
vaccination campaign before the Omicron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:14.640
variant appeared. Missing that window has&nbsp;
resulted in the struggle it’s facing today.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.920
That wasn't really planning for&nbsp;
the future and wasn't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.400
planning for the contingency of a more&nbsp;
transmissible variant as we've seen.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:28.800
On top of that, there’s the question of&nbsp;
vaccine efficacy. Research has shown that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:35.520
the Chinese-made, inactivated-virus vaccines are&nbsp;
less effective against infections with Omicron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:40.400
than mRNA vaccines, like, for&nbsp;
example, the Pfizer-BioNTech vaccine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.560
But China still hasn’t approved the use of&nbsp;
foreign-made vaccines for its population.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.600
Instead, it is betting on&nbsp;
home-grown mRNA vaccines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.920
which are still undergoing clinical trials.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.600
And that seems to be driven purely&nbsp;
by political decisions, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:05.360
political motives to not want to be seen,&nbsp;
to be reliant on international support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:11.600
international science and technology, but to be&nbsp;
able to develop their own vaccines and use them.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:14.720
This possibility of high&nbsp;
death rates and infections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:21.200
if China drops its zero-COVID approach is&nbsp;
just too risky for the ruling Communist Party.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:31.360
A lot of how people see COVID, in 2020, 2021,&nbsp;
is in comparison with the West. So in countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:37.120
like the United States and Western European&nbsp;
countries, COVID rates were very high, death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:44.960
rates were very high, people had waves of lockdown&nbsp;
and a lot of political disturbances. And it's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:50.720
felt like chaos in the West, whereas China was&nbsp;
relatively peaceful and had very low death rates.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.840
Professor Nancy Qian is currently based in the US.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.420
She has been observing and writing&nbsp;
about China’s COVID strategy.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:04.960
And I think that's what the politicians&nbsp;
want to avoid and they just don't know how.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:10.640
They can’t avoid it completely. They're trying&nbsp;
to find the path of minimal political cost.&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.480
And the political cost of abandoning&nbsp;
the ‘zero-COVID’ strategy currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:21.120
seems to be too high. It has become&nbsp;
President Xi’s signature policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:28.560
He is expected to announce his intention for a&nbsp;
landmark third term at China’s 20th Party Congress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:35.680
coming up in September – So it’s bad timing for&nbsp;
a gamble on a new way to handle the pandemic.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:42.160
They think that they have demonstrated the&nbsp;
superiority of the system in containing the virus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:46.800
And therefore, they would like to continue with&nbsp;
that. And they think they can continue with that.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:50.480
But the costs of continuing&nbsp;
with the policy are growing.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.640
The stringent COVID strategy is taking its&nbsp;
toll – not only on people’s daily lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:02.880
but also on China’s economy. And that too&nbsp;
will tarnish the ruling Party’s popularity.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.080
Is there a way, then, for China&nbsp;
to both transition out of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.840
zero-COVID strategy and save the Party’s image?

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:18.800
I believe that it should be possible for them&nbsp;
to find a face-saving way to message this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.640
It's all about the messaging, to say:&nbsp;
Look, there is going to be some risks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.120
but if we don't open up, there are other types&nbsp;
of risks and other costs that we have to pay.

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:31.040
The difficulty is that the longer&nbsp;
they go on as they are now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.680
the harder it will be for them to do that&nbsp;
messaging effectively. Because the longer they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:39.840
go on like they do now, the more unhappy people&nbsp;
are and the less people trust the government.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:46.560
And this all leaves Xi Jinping and the Communist&nbsp;
party in a particularly tricky situation.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:51.760
They do just seem to think that they can keep&nbsp;
testing and containing their way out of this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:58.160
However long term, It doesn't&nbsp;
look very sustainable.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:07.840
There is going to be a political hit&nbsp;
no matter what. They can’t escape it.

